#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# specularity

#% text
# `specularity(c, n)` is a directive of 3D graphics: a shine of colour `c` and exponent `n` on the faces that follow.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The 3D renderer shades faces with its own fixed light; `specularity` is accepted in graphics and plot options, so code written for other lighting runs, and is not drawn.

#% item
# The call stays as it is when evaluated alone.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
graphics3d([specularity(white, 20), orange, sphere()])
#% output
#= image("specularity.files/specularity-1.svgz")

#% cell
plot3d(x^2 - y^2, [x, -1, 1], [y, -1, 1], plot_style: [specularity(white, 50)])
#% output
#= image("specularity.files/specularity-2.svgz")

#% cell
specularity(white, 20)
#% output
#= specularity(white, 20)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The picture is the same without it:

#% cell
graphics3d([orange, sphere()])
#% output
#= image("specularity.files/specularity-4.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `graphics3d`, `plot3d`, `lighting`, `glow`
